2.4.m:
Protector of the destitute, Maheśa, son of Śaṅkara, Lord of the three worlds, O Lord, Master of illusion, I have come for refuge; protect me, lover of Brahmins!
You are the Lord of all, praised by the gods headed by Brahmā who know all bows;
you assume an illusory form, granting joy to your devotees, intent on protection, user of illusion!

Then rivers flowed there by hundreds carrying blood; / bhūtas, pretas, and others gathered there by the hundreds.

Jackals and she-jackals devoured much flesh there, / as well as vultures, hawks, and meat-eating crows;

indeed, they ate the abundant flesh of the fallen. (30)
